  "notes": "Surfnerd overview: Kahalu'u Bay is a mellow reef break with a handful of rolling peaks to choose from, breaking over a mix of lava, rock, and coral. Most of the time it serves up forgiving rights and lefts that suit beginners, but when a solid swell fills in, the outermost peak turns on with a semi-steep drop and rippable lines that keep intermediate surfers busy. The surroundings are pristine. Skill (surfnerd): Beginner, Intermediate, Expert. Surf-forecast Kahalu-u: \"Kahalu'u in Big Island is an exposed reef break that is often working and works all around the year. The best wind direction is from the east southeast. Tends to receive distant groundswells and the best swell direction is from the west. The quality of the surf isn't affected by the tide. When it's working here, it can get crowded. Hazards include - Urchins - Rips / undertow - Rocks - Localism.\" Surf-forecast Kahaluu-Beach-Park: \"Kahalu'u Beach Park in Big Island is an exposed reef break that has reliable surf and can work at any time of the year. The best wind direction is from the east. Tends to receive distant groundswells and the ideal swell angle is from the southwest. Reef breaks provide lefts and rights. Surfable at all stages of the tide. It's sometimes crowded here. Beware of urchins, rips, rocks and locals.\" Wannasurf kahalu_u: quality Regional Classic; experience All surfers; wave Reef-coral, Right and left; swell size Starts working at Less than 1m / 3ft and holds up to 3m+ / 10ft+; crowd weekday Few surfers, weekend Crowded. Access (surfnerd): Getting in is easy, with parking right at Kahalu'u Bea"
