  "notes": "Surfnerd overview: Mahaiula Bay is a right-hand reef break tucked inside Kekaha Kai State Park, one of a handful of quiet waves peeling over dead coral in pristine, lava-framed surroundings. When a west swell fills in, the wave wakes up and breaks hard and fast. Skill (surfnerd): Intermediate. Surf-forecast Mahaiula-Bay: \"Mahai'ula Bay in Big Island is a fairly exposed beach break that has reasonably consistent surf. Winter is the favoured time of year for surfing here. The best wind direction is from the east southeast. Groundswells are more common than windswells and the ideal swell direction is from the northwest. Prevailing right breaking waves at the beach break. Good surf at all stages of the tide. Sometimes crowded. Take care of rocks in the line up.\" Wannasurf mahaiula_bay: quality Regional Classic; experience All surfers; wave Point-break, Right; swell size Starts working at  and holds up to; crowd weekday Few surfers, weekend Crowded; page has no GPS position. Access (surfnerd): Kekaha Kai State Park is the way in to this stretch of reef. From here, a long hike north across desolate lava fields leads to the powerful, shifty rights at Makalawena, which don't mind the east trades. Other guide pins from this position: surf-forecast Mahaiula-Bay 0.7 km."
