  "notes": "Surfnerd overview: Hapuna Point sits at the southern edge of Hapuna Bay, a few miles south of Kawaihae Harbor on the Big Island, fronting one of the island's largest and whitest sand beaches. Most of the time this is a beach shorepound breaking over sand, with an outer wave that breaks over shallow, uneven reef. It isn't the standout surf spot on the coast, but it can turn on: a big winter swell transforms the bay's southern edge into a spectacular left-hand point. Skill (surfnerd): Expert. Surf-forecast Hapuna: \"Hapuna in Big Island is a reasonably exposed beach break that has quite reliable surf. Winter is the best time of year for surfing here. Offshore winds blow from the east northeast. Tends to receive distant groundswells and the best swell direction is from the northwest. The beach breaks offer lefts and rights. Sometimes crowded. Beware of rips and sharks.\" Wannasurf hapuna: quality Normal; experience All surfers; wave Sand-bar, Right and left; swell size Starts working at 2.5m-3m / 8ft-10ft and holds up to 4m+ / 12ft; crowd weekday Few surfers, weekend Crowded. Access (surfnerd): Getting in is easy: it's straight off the highway, with parking at Hapuna Beach Park. Other guide pins from this position: surf-forecast Hapuna 0.6 km, wannasurf hapuna 0.2 km. wannasurf pin 0.2 km from ours. surfnerd pin 0.1 km from ours. surf-forecast pin 0.7 km from ours (its coordinates are given to 0.01°). surf-forecast: \"reasonably exposed beach\". Types differ (surfnerd Reef; surf-forecast reasonably exposed beach); the non-beach type is recorded."
